# `optim::SequentialQPOptions`

Settings of the sequential quadratic programming algorithm: limits, tolerances, curvature model, linear algebra and scaling.

Used when `OptimaOptions::algorithm` is `SequentialQP`, through `OptimaOptions::sequential_qp`. At each iteration the algorithm builds a quadratic model of the problem around the current point, solves it for a step, and takes a fraction of that step chosen by a line search on a penalty merit function. The fields below fall in four groups: the iteration budget and tolerances that decide when to stop; the line search and merit penalty (`armijo`, `backtracking`, `initial_penalty`, second-order corrections); the model (`hessian_approximation`, `lbfgs_memory`); and the linear algebra of the subproblem (`linear_system_solver`, `system_structure`, regularisation, Krylov settings). `problem_scaling` rescales the problem automatically. Most problems need none of these changed; the usual ones to touch are `max_iterations`, the two tolerances and `hessian_approximation`.

## Fields

### `max_iterations`

`std::int64_t`, default `100`.

The iteration budget. A run that uses it all ends `MaxIterations`.

### `max_line_search_iterations`

`std::int64_t`, default `20`.

The most step reductions tried per iteration.

### `max_second_order_corrections`

`std::int64_t`, default `2`.

The most corrections tried when a full step is rejected only because it breaks a nonlinear constraint.

### `max_restoration_iterations`

`std::int64_t`, default `8`.

The iteration cap of the restoration phase that pulls an infeasible point back toward feasibility.

### `max_restoration_line_search_iterations`

`std::int64_t`, default `20`.

Line-search steps within one restoration iteration.

### `initial_step_size`

`double`, default `1.0`.

The first trial fraction of the step (1 is the full step).

### `backtracking`

`double`, default `0.5`.

The factor by which a rejected step fraction is multiplied.

### `armijo`

`double`, default `1e-4`.

The sufficient-decrease constant of the line search.

### `initial_penalty`

`double`, default `10.0`.

The starting weight of the constraint violation in the merit function; raised as needed.

### `constraint_tolerance`

`double`, default `1e-6`.

The largest violation accepted as feasible.

### `optimality_tolerance`

`double`, default `1e-6`.

The stationarity accepted as optimal, relative to the size of the starting gradient.

### `step_tolerance`

`double`, default `1e-6`.

Steps shorter than this count as no progress.

### `value_progress_tolerance`

`double`, default `1e-6`.

Objective changes smaller than this count as no progress.

### `active_set_tolerance`

`double`, default `1e-4`.

A constraint within this of its limit is treated as active.

### `regularization`

`double`, default `1e-6`.

The first diagonal shift tried when a factorisation fails; grown tenfold per retry.

### `bfgs_curvature_tolerance`

`double`, default `1e-8`.

A curvature pair is used by the BFGS update only if its curvature exceeds this.

### `second_order_correction_tolerance`

`double`, default `1e-4`.

A rejected step is corrected only if its violation exceeds this.

### `fraction_to_boundary`

`double`, default `0.995`.

How close to the boundary of the subproblem's inequalities a step may go.

### `pivot_threshold`

`double`, default `1e-12`.

The smallest pivot a factorisation accepts.

### `max_krylov_iterations`

`std::int64_t`, default `0`.

The cap of conjugate-gradient iterations; 0 chooses automatically.

### `iterative_dimension_threshold`

`std::int64_t`, default `3000`.

At this many unknowns the subproblem switches to conjugate gradients.

### `krylov_tolerance`

`double`, default `1e-2`.

The relative tolerance of the iterative solves (capped at 1e-8).

### `krylov_absolute_tolerance`

`double`, default `1e-10`.

The absolute tolerance of the iterative solves.

### `objective_limit`

`double`, default `-inf`.

Stop with `ObjectiveTargetReached` once a feasible objective falls below this.

### `project_bounds`

`bool`, default `true`.

Clip iterates back inside the bounds.

### `enable_restoration`

`bool`, default `true`.

Allow the restoration phase.

### `system_structure`

`optim::OptimaLinearSystemStructure`, default `Auto`.

Dense or sparse treatment of the subproblem matrices.

### `linear_system_solver`

`optim::OptimaLinearSystemSolver`, default `Auto`.

How the reduced subproblem system is solved.

### `hessian_approximation`

`optim::OptimaHessianApproximation`, default `Bfgs`.

The curvature model of the quadratic subproblem.

### `lbfgs_memory`

`std::int64_t`, default `10`.

The number of step pairs kept by the limited-memory model.

### `hvp_options`

`diff::HvpOptions`, default `{}`.

Settings of the Hessian-vector products used by `Exact` and `HessianVector`.

### `problem_scaling`

`optim::OptimaProblemScaling`, default `ObjectiveAndConstraints`.

Whether and how the problem is rescaled before solving.

### `progress_hook`

`std::function<bool(const optim::OptimaProgressSnapshot &)>`, default `empty`.

Called each iteration; return `true` to stop.

## Guidance

Set through `OptimaOptions::sequential_qp`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Tolerances set how long the run goes on. The Rosenbrock valley `(1−a)² + 100(b−a²)²` from `(−1.2, 1)`: a loose optimality tolerance stops early, a tight one follows the valley to the minimum at `(1, 1)`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(8));
  const auto rosenbrock = [](const pluss::Series &x) {
    const pluss::Series a = x.select(0, 0), b = x.select(0, 1);
    return (1.0 - a).square() + 100.0 * (b - a.square()).square();
  };
  for (const double tolerance : {1e-2, 1e-8}) {
    optim::OptimaOptions options;
    options.sequential_qp.optimality_tolerance = tolerance;
    const optim::OptimaState result = optim::optima(rosenbrock, pluss::series::make({-1.2, 1.0}), options);
    out.writeln("tolerance", tolerance, ": iterations =", result.iteration, ", x =", result.params.at("x").items<double>());
  }
}
```

### Example 2

Curvature models on the same valley. The default BFGS builds curvature from gradients; `Exact` takes the exact Hessian of the Lagrangian and needs the fewest iterations; `Identity` uses no curvature and is far too slow here.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <utility>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto rosenbrock = [](const pluss::Series &x) {
    const pluss::Series a = x.select(0, 0), b = x.select(0, 1);
    return (1.0 - a).square() + 100.0 * (b - a.square()).square();
  };
  const std::pair<const char *, optim::OptimaHessianApproximation> models[] = {
      {"Bfgs", optim::OptimaHessianApproximation::Bfgs}, {"Lbfgs", optim::OptimaHessianApproximation::Lbfgs},
      {"Exact", optim::OptimaHessianApproximation::Exact}, {"Identity", optim::OptimaHessianApproximation::Identity}};
  for (const auto &[name, model] : models) {
    optim::OptimaOptions options;
    options.sequential_qp.hessian_approximation = model;
    const optim::OptimaState result = optim::optima(rosenbrock, pluss::series::make({-1.2, 1.0}), options);
    out.writeln(name, ": iterations =", result.iteration, ", exit =", optim::optima_exit_flag_name(result.exit_flag));
  }
}
```

### Example 3

An iteration budget. With `max_iterations = 2` the run ends `MaxIterations`; the point is the best so far.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const pluss::Series target = pluss::series::make({1.0, 2.0});
  const auto objective = [&](const pluss::Series &x) { return (x - target).square().sum(); };
  const auto disc = optim::constraints().nl_ineq([](const Pack &p) { return p.at("x").square().sum() - 1.0; });

  optim::OptimaOptions options;
  options.sequential_qp.max_iterations = 2;
  const optim::OptimaState result = optim::optima(objective, pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0}), options, disc);
  out.writeln("exit =", optim::optima_exit_flag_name(result.exit_flag), ", iterations =", result.iteration, ", evaluations =", result.function_evaluations);
  out.writeln("x =", result.params.at("x").items<double>());
}
```

### Example 4

Automatic problem scaling. `None` solves the problem as written; the default `ObjectiveAndConstraints` rescales each function so one tolerance fits all. An objective multiplied by 1e6 converges either way and returns the same point; the scaled run reaches the objective `1e6 · 1.527864` more exactly.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(2));
  const pluss::Series target = pluss::series::make({1.0, 2.0});

  const auto big = [&](const pluss::Series &x) { return 1.0e6 * (x - target).square().sum(); };
  for (const optim::OptimaProblemScaling scaling : {optim::OptimaProblemScaling::None, optim::OptimaProblemScaling::ObjectiveAndConstraints}) {
    optim::OptimaOptions options;
    options.sequential_qp.problem_scaling = scaling;
    const optim::OptimaState result = optim::optima(big, pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0}), options,
        optim::constraints().nl_ineq([](const Pack &p) { return p.at("x").square().sum() - 1.0; }));
    out.writeln(optim::optima_problem_scaling_name(scaling), ": exit =", optim::optima_exit_flag_name(result.exit_flag), ", objective =", result.evaluation.value.item<double>());
  }
}
```

### Example 5

Watching a run with a hook inside the algorithm's own options.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const pluss::Series target = pluss::series::make({1.0, 2.0});
  const auto objective = [&](const pluss::Series &x) { return (x - target).square().sum(); };
  const auto disc = optim::constraints().nl_ineq([](const Pack &p) { return p.at("x").square().sum() - 1.0; });

  optim::OptimaOptions options;
  options.sequential_qp.progress_hook = [&out](const optim::OptimaProgressSnapshot &s) {
    if (s.stage == optim::OptimaProgressStage::Iteration) out.writeln("iteration", s.iteration, ": violation =", s.evaluation.max_constraint_violation);
    return false;
  };
  const optim::OptimaState result = optim::optima(objective, pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0}), options, disc);
}
```

